<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Coosa River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Coosa River flows about 450 km from north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>, where it joins the Tallapoosa to form the Alabama River. Its valley was home to the Mississippian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdom</a> of Coosa, visited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernando-de-soto">Hernando de Soto</a> in 1540, and later to Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> towns. It is now dammed for hydroelectric power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/coosa-river.jpg" alt="Coosa River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mike Cline · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FallColorsOnTheCoosaRiver.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Coosa River begins in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, at the confluence of the Oostanaula and Etowah rivers, and around 90% of its length lies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>. The Oostanaula is itself formed by the Conasauga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coosawattee-river">Coosawattee Rivers</a>. The Coosa ends just north-east of Montgomery, the state capital, where it meets the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tallapoosa-river">Tallapoosa River</a> south of Wetumpka. Shoals and waterfalls along its lowest 65 miles, among them the Devil's Staircase, long kept boats on the upper river from reaching the Alabama River and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Native Americans had lived in the Coosa Valley for millennia before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernando-de-soto">Hernando de Soto</a> and his men arrived, and the Coosa chiefdom was then one of the most powerful in the south-east. More than a century after the Spanish left, the British built strong trading ties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Muscogee Creek</a> bands of the valley around the late 17th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, whose settlements on the coast included Mobile, saw the river as a gateway to the whole South and contested the valley until it gave up its lands east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1763.</p><p>By the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> held the lower valley and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> the upper. After the Creek defeat at the Battle of Horseshoe Bend, the Treaty of Fort Jackson of 1814 forced the Creek to cede a large amount of land, leaving them a reserve between the Coosa and Tallapoosa rivers. In the 1820s and 1830s the Creek and Cherokee were removed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a>; the Cherokee capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-echota">New Echota</a>, had stood on the river's headwater tributaries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. Settlers then filled the valley with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> plantations worked by enslaved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a>.</p><p>The first river town of the basin grew at the foot of the Devil's Staircase and took the native name Wetumpka, meaning rumbling waters or falling stream. Riverboats used some 200 miles of the upper river as a commercial waterway into the early 20th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Most of the river is impounded, and Alabama Power, a unit of the Southern Company, owns seven dams and powerhouses on it. While building the first of them, Lay, Mitchell and Jordan, the company pioneered methods of controlling malaria that the Medical Division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> came to study. The dams have harmed several species found only in the Coosa, a system that has already lost a significant portion of its aquatic fauna to extinction. Cities on its banks include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, Gadsden and Wetumpka.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Coosa River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its people moved down the <b>Coosa River</b> into Alabama, where the town of Coosa became a leading town of the Upper Muscogee, part of the Muscogee Confederacy.</blockquote><a href="/state/coosa-chiefdom">Coosa chiefdom</a></li></ul></section>
